<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Jalayirids</h1><p class="st-meta">1335 CE – 1432 CE (approx.) · capital Baghdad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mongol dynasty that ruled Iraq and Azerbaijan after the Ilkhanate, whose court at Baghdad and Tabriz fostered a golden age of Persian painting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalairs">Jalayirs</a> were a Mongol tribe prominent in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-said-bahadur-khan">Abu Sa'id</a> died in 1335 without an heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-buzurg">Hasan Buzurg</a> ('Hasan the Tall') set up puppet khans and by 1336 to 1340 controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, fighting the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chupanids">Chupanids</a> for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Hasan's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-uways">Shaykh Uways</a> (1356 to 1374) took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> in 1360 and ruled both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a> presented themselves as heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhans</a> and patronised Persian culture: the poet Salman Savaji praised them, and Jalayirid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> painters, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junayd-painter">Junayd</a>, shaped the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid</a> style. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-jalayir">Ahmad Jalayir</a>, a poet and calligrapher himself, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> against repeated attacks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> drove Ahmad from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1393 and 1401. Ahmad returned but was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qara-yusuf">Qara Yusuf</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu</a> in 1410. Jalayirid remnants held parts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> until 1432.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jalayirids.jpg" alt="Jalayirids" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Demis Map Server · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jalayirids,_Muzaffarids_in_1374.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1335 CE – 1432 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baghdad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasan Buzurg, Shaykh Uways, Ahmad Jalayir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>, <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jalayirid Sultanate, Jalayir dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=jalayirids" aria-label="Jalayirids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5814 968 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jalayirids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6134 1224L6134 1224M6134 1224L6153 1065M6134 1224L6582 1190M6134 1224L6153 1065"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M6153 1065h0M6582 1190h0M6153 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Baghdad. <a href="/atlas?year=1384&amp;focus=jalayirids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
